---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/languages/foundational/how-to-learn-any-language">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Languages, and it assumes nothing but the experience of
    speaking one language already — any language. You do not need to have been good at school
    language classes; in fact, this lesson spends some of its time explaining why classroom
    grammar drilling feels different from real acquisition. Everything technical is built here,
    and the lesson is deliberately method-shaped: it teaches you how to learn, because every
    later lesson in this subject assumes the habit system this one sets up.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain what comprehensible input is, and why material slightly past your level is the engine of acquisition;</li>
    <li>use spaced repetition deliberately — frequency-first decks, cards from your own input — to make vocabulary permanent;</li>
    <li>explain why early speaking accelerates learning and why pronunciation is the stubborn part, down to what happened to your ears in infancy;</li>
    <li>read the vocabulary-versus-hours curve, and interpret the plateau as a shape of the language, not a verdict on your talent;</li>
    <li>say what CEFR level A1 ("Breakthrough") concretely requires, and run a daily system sized to reach it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit runs through the lesson: track inputs, not outcomes. Hours listened, cards
    matured, sessions spoken — the scoreboard you can control. The outcomes follow the curve.
  </p>

  <h2>Languages are caught, not taught</h2>
  <p>
    You already know how languages are learned, from the inside. Nobody drilled you on the
    rules of your first language; you heard tens of thousands of hours of it being used to get
    real things done, and the system assembled itself. Second-language research has spent half
    a century turning that observation into a method, and the method underlying nearly every
    successful learner has three load-bearing parts: massive <strong>comprehensible
    input</strong> (listening and reading just past your level — Stephen Krashen's "i+1"),
    <strong>spaced repetition</strong> for the words that must stick, and <strong>early
    speaking</strong> to force retrieval. Talent is mostly hours, arranged well. This lesson
    explains each part — why it works, what it looks like in practice — and then anchors it to
    the framework your syllabus actually uses: the CEFR levels, starting with A1, the
    "Breakthrough" level you can reach in weeks rather than years.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="true or false — how acquisition actually runs" widget="judge" config={htlalEnginesJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Flip the four statements and check them against your own first language: nobody drilled you
    on its rules — you absorbed it from tens of thousands of hours of real use. Notice the
    false card's reframe too: talent is mostly hours, arranged well — input, spaced
    repetition and early speaking, the three engines this lesson builds.
  </p>

  <h2>The curve every learner rides</h2>
  <p>
    Before the method, the landscape. Vocabulary acquisition follows a curve that is honest and
    a little cruel, and the plot below draws it: words known against hours of practice. The
    first few hundred hours buy a huge share of everyday communication, because languages are
    wildly uneven in word value — a small core of high-frequency words does most of the work.
    The top 1,000 words typically cover the large majority of ordinary speech, and the most
    frequent 2,000–3,000 push past 90% of everyday text and talk. Then gains slow as you chase
    rarer words.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="vocabulary versus hours of practice — early words come fast; the plateau is where methods matter" widget="plotter" config={vocabCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve shoots up, then flattens. Slide the "target
    vocabulary" dial and the plateau moves — but the shape never changes, because the shape is
    the frequency distribution of the language itself, not a property of you. Slide the "early
    gains" dial to feel what better or worse study habits do to the first hundred hours. The
    flat stretch is where most learners quit — usually because they misread it as evidence they
    lack talent.
  </p>
  <p>
    The plateau deserves respect, so let us size it. Vocabulary researcher Paul Nation's
    estimates put <em>comfortable reading</em> — knowing 98% of the words on a page — at
    roughly 8,000–9,000 word families for a language like English. The gap between "can order
    dinner" and "can read a novel" is that plateau. Knowing <em>why</em> it exists keeps you
    going: the early words were frequent enough to meet thirty times a day; the late ones need
    deliberate hunting.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="call the plateau's size" widget="estimate" config={htlalPlateauEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Estimate the plateau's size before revealing: roughly 8,000–9,000 word families for
    comfortable reading — knowing 98 per cent of the words on a page, by Paul Nation's
    estimates. Notice the arithmetic underneath the gap: the early words were frequent
    enough to meet thirty times a day, while the late ones need deliberate hunting.
  </p>

  <h2>Comprehensible input: the engine</h2>
  <p>
    Krashen's <strong>input hypothesis</strong> (1982) claims acquisition happens when you
    understand messages slightly above your current level — <em>i + 1</em>, where <em>i</em> is
    what you already have. The mechanism is meaning-first: your brain is an excellent pattern
    extractor when it cares what the sentence is for. Watching a show you 80–90% understand,
    your attention is held by the story while the 10–20% unknown gets consistently surrounded
    by enough context to be absorbed — the unknown word sits inside known grammar, next to
    known words, doing an inferable job.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is why <em>graded readers</em> exist (real stories rewritten with a limited
    vocabulary), why children's content is a legitimate adult strategy, and why re-watching a
    familiar show with target-language audio works: you already know the plot, so every scrap
    of attention is free for the language. The practical corollary is the 80–90% rule: material
    that is <em>too</em> hard (you understand 40%) produces noise, frustration and no
    acquisition; material too easy produces comfort and no growth. Daily slightly-too-hard
    input — podcasts at walking pace, subtitled shows, graded news — is the single
    highest-yield habit in the field. And input must be <em>massive</em>: hours per week, not
    minutes, because the curve above is driven by exposure volume.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pick tonight's input" widget="branch" config={htlalInputBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Branch the evening's thirty minutes and read the middle card: the episode you understand
    80–90% of is the pick — the story holds attention while the unknown 10–20 per cent sits
    surrounded by known grammar, doing an inferable job. Notice what the walk then demands:
    make it a routine, because hours per week rather than minutes is what drives the curve.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> studying grammar tables is acquisition. It is not — grammar
  knowledge helps you notice patterns in input, but the patterns themselves are absorbed from
  contact, not memorised as rules. The test is classic: a learner can recite a tense rule
  perfectly and still fail to use it in conversation, because the conversational system runs on
  absorbed chunks, not conscious grammar. Study grammar to understand what you hear; acquire
  grammar by hearing it.</p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the line the grammar-table myth would love" widget="spot-error" config={htlalGrammarSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Find the line the grammar-table myth would love: drilling tables to automaticity is not what
    acquisition is made of — perfect recitation of a verb form can coexist with failure to
    use it mid-conversation. Notice the correction's two-sided advice: study grammar to
    understand what you hear, and acquire grammar by hearing it.
  </p>

  <h2>Spaced repetition: making words permanent</h2>
  <p>
    Input supplies words; <strong>spaced repetition (SRS)</strong> keeps them. The psychology
    is the forgetting curve: memory decays exponentially, but each successful retrieval — and
    especially each retrieval that happens <em>just as the memory begins to fade</em> —
    flattens the decay dramatically. SRS software schedules exactly that: a card returns in 1
    day, then 3, then 7, then 16, each interval stretched by your success. The result is
    remarkable efficiency: 15–20 minutes of daily cards can maintain thousands of words,
    because the system spends its time only on words about to be lost.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="which line is the SRS card?" widget="trace" config={htlalCurveTrace} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Trace the two cards and pick the flatter line: each successful retrieval, especially one
    timed just as the memory begins to fade, stretches the next interval — returns at 1 day,
    then 3, then 7, then 16. Notice the efficiency that follows: 15–20 minutes of daily
    cards can maintain thousands of words.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two refinements make SRS powerful rather than merely tidy. Learn <strong>frequency
    first</strong>: build or import decks of the most common 1,000–2,000 words before anything
    else, because each of those words returns value every single day. And make cards from
    <em>your own</em> input — the word you met in last night's episode, with its sentence —
    because context is what the word actually lives in. Vocabulary without context is a phone
    book; with context it is speech.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> So far: input supplies the words and spaced repetition keeps them — frequency-first decks of
    the most common 1,000–2,000 words, and cards made from your own input, because context
    is what a word actually lives in.
  </p>

  <h2>Output: speaking before you're ready</h2>
  <p>
    The third engine is <strong>output</strong>, and its value is different from input's.
    Producing language <em>forces retrieval</em> — reaching for a word you have seen but never
    used, and successfully grabbing it, does more to fix that word than ten more sightings.
    Speaking also exposes gaps instantly: the moment you cannot say "I need to leave early
    tomorrow" is a precise map of what to learn next, which is why conversation partners and
    tutors function as accelerator, not merely practice. The standard advice — <em>speak
    before you feel ready</em> — follows from the psychology of errors: a mistake corrected in
    conversation is the fastest teacher available, and perfectionist delay protects your ego at
    the cost of the hours.
  </p>
  <p>
    Add deliberate <strong>shadowing</strong> (repeating audio a half-second behind the
    speaker) for pronunciation, because accent is a motor skill: your mouth needs the same
    hundreds of repetitions as your ears. Learn the <strong>script properly</strong> if there
    is one — Devanagari, kana, Hangul — using stroke order and mnemonics; Hangul's design is
    famously learnable in a weekend, and reading is the highest-bandwidth input channel you
    will ever own.
  </p>

  <h2>Why pronunciation is the stubborn part</h2>
  <p>
    Accent has a reputation for being fixed by talent, and the science says something more
    interesting: it is mostly about categories your ears set up in infancy. Newborn babies can
    discriminate the sound contrasts of <em>all</em> the world's languages — researchers can
    measure this with a habituation trick, watching whether a bored, sucking baby perks up when
    one syllable switches to another. But the categories tune fast: in one classic study,
    six-month-old babies from English-speaking homes noticed contrasts from English, Hindi and
    a Salish language equally well (80–90% success); by ten months success on the non-native
    contrasts had fallen to about 50–60%, and by their first birthday to 10–20% — matching
    English-speaking adults. One year of listening, and the ears have been sorted into
    English.
  </p>
  <p>
    What does that mean for you as an adult? Psycholinguist Catherine Best's theory predicts
    which new-language contrasts will be easy and which hard, and experiments with English
    speakers learning Zulu sounds confirmed the pattern. Easy: contrasts that <em>map onto</em>
    one you already make (English listeners told apart two Zulu lateral fricatives differing in
    voicing about 95% correctly, because English has many voiced–voiceless pairs), and
    contrasts that are <em>entirely new</em> (Zulu clicks, about 80% — no English category gets
    in the way, so you can build fresh ones). Hard: two foreign sounds that both land in a
    <em>single</em> category of your native language — English listeners managed only about 65%
    on Zulu's plosive-versus-implosive bilabial stops, because both resemble English "b". This
    is the famous case of Japanese speakers and English "l" versus "r": Japanese has one sound
    category where English has two, so the learner must carve one lifelong category into two.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each contrast case with the measured result" widget="match" config={htlalContrastMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each case with its measured outcome and read the difficulty gradient: contrasts mapping
    onto a category you already make score about 95 per cent, entirely new sounds about 80,
    but two foreign sounds landing in a single native category manage only about 65. Notice
    what the gradient proves: the ear's difficulty is categorical, and categories can be
    trained.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical consequence is encouraging. If the difficulty is categorical, it can be
    trained: minimal-pair listening drills ("rock" or "lock?") teach your ears to attend to the
    difference your native categories blur, and shadowing then trains the mouth to produce it.
    Progress is measured in intelligibility, not nativeness — and intelligibility responds to
    practice at any age.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if you do not start young, decent pronunciation is
  hopeless." The infant-tuning research explains why native-like accent is rarer in adult
  learners — the categories hardened — but it also shows the difficulty is specific and
  trainable. Adults regularly reach clear, comfortable accents in a second language with
  deliberate listening and mouth practice; what they rarely reach is the accent nobody can
  place. Aim at being understood effortlessly, and the ceiling is far higher than folklore
  says.</p>

  <h2>A1, concretely: what "Breakthrough" means</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>CEFR scale</strong> (Council of Europe, 2001) describes languages by what you
    can <em>do</em>, not by courses completed: A1 Breakthrough, A2 Waystage, B1 Threshold, B2
    Vantage, C1 Effective Proficiency, C2 Mastery. A1 is the syllabus unit this lesson anchors
    to, and its descriptors are worth reading as a shopping list, because they are startlingly
    achievable. The first: <em>understand and use familiar everyday expressions and very basic
    phrases aimed at the satisfaction of needs of a concrete type</em> — greetings, numbers,
    prices, "where is...?", "I would like...", ordering food, asking directions. The second:
    <em>introduce yourself and others, and ask and answer questions about personal details —
    where you live, people you know, things you have</em> — which is a memorisable-and-
    personalised mini-speech plus a bank of question forms ("What's your name? Where are you
    from? Do you have brothers or sisters?").
  </p>
  <p>
    The third descriptor is the one beginners underrate: <em>interact in a simple way provided
    the other person talks slowly and clearly and is prepared to help</em> — the crucial clause
    being the last one. A1 is a <em>cooperative</em> conversation, and learning the repair kit
    ("slower, please", "how do you say...?", "can you repeat that?") is itself A1 content,
    because it keeps the conversation alive past your vocabulary. Hours-wise, language schools
    typically budget on the order of 60–100 guided hours for A1 in a "close" language for an
    English speaker — far more for a distant one — but the honest unit is the curve's: input
    hours, arranged by the method above.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> adults are worse language learners than children. Adults
  are <em>different</em> learners. They have worse accents more often (the ear categories from
  the last section) and less free time, but they learn vocabulary and structure
  <em>faster</em>, can use literacy and metacognition, and can study deliberately. The
  "critical period" applies most sharply to native-like accent; the rest is hours. A1 in eight
  disciplined weeks is an ordinary adult outcome, not a gifted one.</p>

  <h2>Building the daily system</h2>
  <p>
    Method becomes progress only as a schedule, and the working shape is small-and-daily rather
    than long-and-weekly — the forgetting curve punishes gaps far more than it rewards
    marathons. A realistic A1 sprint on a close language: 20 minutes of SRS (frequency decks
    first), 30–45 minutes of comprehensible input (a graded reader at breakfast, a subtitled
    episode at night), and 2–3 conversations per week with a tutor or exchange partner, plus
    shadowing five minutes a day for the motor skill of pronunciation.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two design rules make the system survive real life. First, <strong>anchor habits to
    existing ones</strong> — cards with morning coffee, input on the commute — because the
    habit, not the willpower, is the fragile part. Second, <strong>track inputs, not
    outcomes</strong>: hours listened, cards matured, sessions spoken. Outcomes (the plateau
    especially) lag weeks behind effort, and learners who watch only outcomes quit precisely
    when the curve is behaving normally; the input ledger is the honest scoreboard. The weekly
    review asks one question — <em>what was hard to say or understand this week?</em> — and
    turns each answer into next week's SRS cards, which is the loop that makes the system
    self-updating: your own failed conversations become your curriculum.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="line up the survival rules" widget="order" config={htlalSystemOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Order the survival rules and find the loop: anchor each habit to an existing one, track the
    inputs you control, name weekly what was hard to say or understand, and turn each answer
    into next week's cards. Notice the widget's diagnosis of the fragile part — the habit,
    not the willpower — and why the input scoreboard keeps you steady while outcomes lag.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the app streak is the learning." Gamified apps are
  excellent schedulers and terrible engines: the volume of comprehensible input they deliver
  is a fraction of what the curve above demands. Use the app for what it is good at — spaced
  review of your cards — and get the bulk of your hours from real shows, books and
  conversations, where the language lives.</p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="bet on what the streak bought" widget="predict" config={htlalStreakPredict} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Lock in a bet before the reveal: the 100-day streak has guaranteed exactly one thing — well-
    spaced review of the cards inside the app, nothing more. Notice what the wrong answers
    cost: comfortable comprehension and conversations with strangers come from real shows,
    books and speakers, where the bulk of the hours must live.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three engines — massive comprehensible input, spaced retrieval,
    brave early output — plus a trained ear for the sounds your first language blurred. What
    remains is to point the system at a concrete first target, and A1 is it.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>You find a podcast you understand about 45% of. Use the 80–90% rule: is this good input, and what will it actually produce?</li>
    <li>Why does an SRS card scheduled for today cost less time than re-reading the whole vocabulary list?</li>
    <li>A friend says: "I am bad at languages — I have been at this for months and I understand less each week." Diagnose what is probably happening on the curve.</li>
    <li>Why do Japanese speakers of English struggle to hear "l" versus "r" — and what drill fixes it?</li>
    <li>Name the three CEFR A1 descriptors in your own words.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) It is below the sweet spot — too much is unknown, so it
    produces noise and frustration rather than acquisition. Step down to graded content until
    comprehension sits around 80–90%. (2) SRS reviews the words on the edge of forgetting and
    skips what you clearly know; re-reading spends equal time on everything, so most of it is
    wasted on material that was never at risk. (3) They have almost certainly left the steep
    part of the curve and entered the plateau — the frequency words are banked and the rare
    ones come slowly. It is the shape of the language, not their talent; the fix is deliberate
    hunting of the words their own reading and listening keep meeting. (4) Japanese built one
    sound category where English has two, so the two English sounds both land in it; the fix is
    minimal-pair listening drills that force the ears to separate the categories, then
    shadowing to train production. (5) Handle everyday concrete needs with set phrases;
    introduce yourself and ask and answer simple personal questions; interact simply as long
    as the partner speaks slowly and helps.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Languages are absorbed through meaningful contact, not memorised: comprehensible input is the engine, spaced repetition the cement, early output the accelerator.</li>
    <li>Seek material you understand 80–90% of — slightly too hard, never hopelessly hard — and get hours per week, not minutes.</li>
    <li>Learn frequency first: the top 1,000–2,000 words pay daily; the 8,000–9,000 word-family plateau is where novels begin.</li>
    <li>The plateau is the shape of the language's frequency distribution, not a verdict on talent.</li>
    <li>Pronunciation difficulty is categorical: your ears sorted the world's sounds into your first language's categories before age one; minimal pairs and shadowing can retrain them enough to be effortlessly understood.</li>
    <li>CEFR A1 means everyday concrete phrases, simple personal introductions and questions, and cooperative simple interaction — reachable in 60–100 guided hours for a close language.</li>
    <li>Small and daily beats long and weekly; anchor habits, track inputs, and turn each week's failures into next week's cards.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The next lesson puts the system to work on the first real target — your first 1,000 words —
    built from frequency lists and your own input. From there the subject climbs the CEFR
    ladder: A2 widens the everyday contexts, B1 reaches the "Threshold" where you survive
    unscripted conversation, and the later lessons handle the skills that A1 only sketches —
    listening strategies, reading growth, and holding your own with a stranger.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four refinements for the committed learner. <strong>Language distance sets the budget:</strong>
    the US Foreign Service Institute's category table is the standard yardstick — roughly
    600–750 class hours for professional proficiency in close languages (Spanish, French,
    Dutch), versus around 2,200 for the "super-hard" group (Japanese, Mandarin, Korean,
    Arabic) — not because those languages are harder in any absolute sense, but because less
    transfers from English; a Mandarin speaker finds Japanese vocabulary nearly free. Choose
    your expectations accordingly, and your first language strategically if you get to choose.
    <strong>Chunks beat words:</strong> much of fluent speech is stored multi-word sequences
    ("to be honest", "on the other hand", the entire phrase "could I have the bill, please?"),
    and learners who collect chunks speak faster and more idiomatically than those who build
    every sentence from vocabulary plus grammar — the finding behind teaching
    <em>collocations</em> explicitly. <strong>Tolerance of ambiguity is trainable:</strong> the
    A1-to-B1 stretch is full of moments where you catch only half the message; learners who can
    keep reading (and keep smiling) through partial understanding accumulate the input that
    resolves the ambiguity later, while those who need certainty stall. <strong>Identity is the
    hidden variable:</strong> research on motivation converged on the idea that learners
    persist when the language is part of who they are becoming — the imagined community they
    speak it with — which is why finding actual people (online tutors, language exchanges, a
    show's fandom) outperforms any app streak. The method beneath every successful learner, in
    one sentence: massive comprehensible input, spaced retrieval, brave early output, and
    enough contact with real speakers to keep the why alive — the curve will take care of the
    rest.
  </p>

